.TH "UBERTOOTH\-DFU" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-dfu\fR \- Device firmware update for Ubertooth
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-dfu [\-d <firmware\.dfu>] [\-u <output\.bin>] [\-r] [\-s <file\.bin>]
[\-U <index|serial>]
ubertooth\-dfu \-\-update\-bootloader \-d <bl_update\.dfu> [\-U <index|serial>]
ubertooth\-dfu [\-U <index|serial>] \-V
ubertooth\-dfu \-h
.fi
.P
Those are the useful forms rather than every accepted command line: the first needs at least one of \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-u\fR, \fB\-r\fR, and \fB\-s\fR, since a command line with no function in it is refused (see \fIARGUMENTS\fR), and \fB\-V\fR and \fB\-h\fR are taken beside anything\.
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
ubertooth\-dfu(1) is a utility for updating firmware on Ubertooth\. It can also download the currently running firmware from the device\. To update firmware, run:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-dfu \-d bluetooth_rxtx\.dfu \-r
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Running the upload or download command automatically resets the device into the firmware update bootloader\. The USB ID of the Ubertooth will change in the bootloader, and this may cause problems when attempting to update firmware from inside a VM\.
.P
Getting a board from its application into DFU is bounded at every step\. A board that refuses the request to enter DFU ends the command there\. The application handle is then closed without the stop command that would conflict with the request just made, and the board is followed by its physical USB bus and port path rather than by an index, so the device found in DFU is the one that was asked\. If none appears and the application device is back, it is asked once more: two attempts in all, each waiting up to five seconds\. Two boards already in DFU are never chosen between\. Each step is timed and named on standard error, so a run shows where its time went (see \fIDIAGNOSTICS\fR)\.
.P
A board that is already waiting in the bootloader is written by a command with no \fB\-U\fR at all, which takes the one board in DFU mode\. The bootloader has no serial number for \fB\-U\fR to name, and is not among the boards an index counts either, so a command with a selector looks for it among the boards running their firmware, does not find it, and says so\. When exactly one board is waiting in DFU, that failure carries a note naming what reaches it: \fB\-d <image> \-r\fR with no \fB\-U\fR writes an image and asks the board to start it, and \fB\-r\fR on its own asks without writing, which is enough for a board that still has an application the bootloader will start\. See ubertooth(7), WHEN A BOARD CANNOT BE USED\.
.P
Every wait for the bootloader is bounded\. Before each operation the tool asks the bootloader for its DFU state and drives it to the idle state, aborting a half\-finished transfer or clearing an error state as needed\. A bootloader that does not answer that question, or that never becomes idle, ends the command with a line naming what it last reported and exit status 1; a board that answers nothing at all usually needs a USB power cycle\.
.P
\fB\-d\fR checks the whole image before the board is asked to leave its application: the 16\-byte DFU suffix and its length, the DFU version, the \fBUFD\fR signature, a non\-empty payload, the suffix CRC, and the suffix \fBidVendor\fR and \fBidProduct\fR, which must name this device (\fB1d50:6003\fR) or be the \fB0xffff\fR wildcard\. Then the firmware info block every image built from this tree carries, at a fixed offset just past the vector table, which says which image the file holds and which revision it was built from:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Checking firmware signature
Image: bluetooth_rxtx 2026\-11\-DEV1+22* (API:<version>)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The API version on that line comes from the block, and the USB \fBbcdDevice\fR \fBubertooth\-util \-V\fR reads off a running board carries the same value, for either image\. This tree's version moves with the firmware, so the samples here write it as \fB<version>\fR; \fBCOMPATIBILITY\.md\fR names the value this tree requires\.
.P
A properly signed DFU file with no readable block there is not an image from this tree, so it is refused rather than written:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: other\-device\.dfu: there is no firmware info block here at offset 0x100; firmware will not be updated
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A file that fails any of these is reported and the command ends with exit status 1 without opening a device, so the board keeps running its firmware\. If a later step fails once the board is already in DFU and \fB\-r\fR was given, the tool detaches it anyway, reopening the DFU device on the same physical path when the failure closed the handle, so the board returns to its application\. A board whose DFU device was seen to leave the bus is left where it is instead: the reset happened, so the handle it answered on is stale and driving it again would report the handle rather than the board\. A board that never answered in DFU at all cannot be detached, and then needs another \fBubertooth\-dfu\fR run or a power cycle\. A reset the bootloader refuses, one it accepts and then does not act on, and one that lands back in the bootloader are all reported and fail the command instead of exiting 0 with the board still in DFU\.
.P
\fB\-d\fR is bounded by the flash the board has as well\. The part identification number is read while the board is still running its application, as it is for \fB\-u\fR, and an image whose blocks would not all fit above the bootloader is refused there, before the board is asked for DFU:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: too\-big\.dfu needs 114944 bytes of flash; part 0x25011722 (LPC1754) has 114688 above the bootloader; firmware will not be updated
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
An image that fits says so, in the same terms as the read\-back (progress, so on standard error, like every line quoted in this page except \fBVerified firmware revision\fR):
.IP "" 4
.nf
The image needs 31488 bytes, 0x4000\-0xbaff: part 0x25011722 (LPC1754) has 128 KiB of flash
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The whole file is written, the 16\-byte DFU suffix included, and the last block is padded out, so the figure compared with the part's flash is the file rounded up to a whole 256\-byte block\. A part this host has no entry for, and one that will not report itself at all, which includes every board already in the bootloader, leave nothing to check against: both say so and write the image as before\.
.P
That check is the only one this tool can act on, because it never reads the board's answer after the stall\. A bootloader built from this tree refuses a block with no flash behind it, bounded the same way as the read\-back described under READING FLASH BACK below, so it stops at the top of the part's flash and says \fBERRADDRESS\fR for the blocks above it; older bootloaders have no upper bound and instead ask the microcontroller's programming interface to erase and program a sector the part does not have, which fails\. Either way everything sent before that point is already written, and all this tool sees of the refusal is the stall, which it reports as \fBerror: could not write block 164: the board's firmware does not support it (stalled, libusb \-9)\fR, a message that says nothing about flash sizes\. Blocks below that point are already written, so the board holds part of an image: the order described below means it holds no vector table either, so there is nothing for it to start\. It is the same cause as the read\-back bound: asking for flash the part has not got\.
.P
A \fB\-d\fR that stops part way through \-\- a cable pulled, a board that leaves the bus, a host that dies \-\- leaves a board with no application to start rather than a broken one\. The first 256 bytes of the application region hold the vector table the board starts from, and they are written twice: blank first, so there is nothing to start while the rest of the image goes in, and with the real vector table in the last step of the transfer\. A bootloader built from this tree finds the blank table, refuses to jump, and waits in DFU for another image, so a flash that failed is followed by another \fBubertooth\-dfu \-d\fR\. A checksum over that table goes in with it, in the word the part reserves for one at offset 0x1c, so a first block whose programming did not finish does not add up and is refused as well\. A board carrying an earlier bootloader jumps into the blank table instead and stops there, so it needs the same trip to the bench it always did; a bootloader reaches a board over ISP, over JTAG, or with \fB\-\-update\-bootloader\fR (see \fIUPDATING THE BOOTLOADER\fR), so this part of the fix reaches a board only when someone puts it there\.
.P
The last step writes a whole 4 KiB sector, because the bootloader erases a flash sector when the first block of it is written, so the vector table goes in before the fifteen blocks that share its sector\. An interruption in between would leave a board a vector table that reads perfectly in front of flash that is only partly written, and the checksum over the table says nothing about it\. So the same step records what the whole download wrote: how many bytes, and a checksum over them, in 32 bytes of the image reserved for it at offset 0xe0\. Those bytes are in the same 256 the vector table is in, so a board that finds a vector table finds the record with it, and a bootloader from this tree adds the image up before it starts any of it\. A sector that is only partly written does not add up \-\- erased flash reads 0xFFFFFFFF \-\- and the board waits in DFU for another image instead\.
.P
That bootloader reads no further than the application region of a 128 KiB part, which is what an Ubertooth One carries and the least any board in the field has read back, because reading flash a part has not got could fault and leave a board for the bench\. A record claiming more than that is not checked, and the image starts on its vector table alone rather than being refused\. The largest image this tree builds, \fBbtbr\fR, is under half the limit, so the one file that reaches it is a whole\-region dump taken off a larger part with \fB\-u\fR and flashed back: that is written and started as it always was, with the vector table checked and the rest of it not\.
.P
The three checks cover different things\. A download that stops between blocks leaves the vector table erased, which the stack pointer and reset vector refuse on their own: the board is handed a block only once all 256 bytes of it have arrived, so a transfer that stops writes nothing of the block it was on\. The checksum over the table covers a first block whose programming did not finish \-\- a board that lost power part way through that one program, which on a bus\-powered board is what a pulled cable does\. The record covers every byte of the image, which is what the other two cannot reach: flash left part way through an erase or a program reads as it pleases\.
.P
A board whose application was put there by other means \-\- a programmer over ISP or JTAG, or another loader \-\- may hold something in that word already\. If it is not a checksum over the table, a bootloader from this tree will not start it, and one \fBubertooth\-dfu \-d\fR puts an image there that it does\. Such an image carries no record either, and a bootloader from this tree then makes neither check and starts it on the stack pointer and reset vector alone, as earlier releases did\.
.P
An image this tree builds leaves the word at offset 0x1c zero and the record at offset 0xe0 empty, and those are the only bytes this tool writes over\. So a read\-back with \fB\-u\fR can differ from the file only inside the four bytes at 0x1c and the fourteen from 0xea to 0xf8, and nowhere else; which of those bytes really differ depends on the image, since some of what goes in is zero too\. An image that already carries the right checksum keeps it, and a record from some earlier download is written over with this one\.
.P
What the detach request returns says nothing about where the board ended up, in either direction\. It can fail because the bootloader resets while the request is still in flight, which on macOS is what every reset that worked looks like, and it can complete on a bootloader that acknowledges it and stays where it is\. So no status from that request is read as a verdict: the tool looks for the DFU device on the same physical path again, and reports the board as detached only once it has gone\. The run then carries on to the \fB\-d \|\.\|\.\|\. \-r\fR verification, which decides whether the flash succeeded\.
.P
One listing that does not find the DFU device is not enough, because the bootloader is absent while the board comes up again whether it left or not\. So every absence is confirmed by a second listing 1\.5 s later, and the board counts as detached only when both agree\. A board that is back on its path by then reset into the bootloader rather than into its application, and that is a failure:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: detach failed: the board reset and came back in the bootloader
note: the firmware was written, so the board holds it; it is in the bootloader rather than running it
note: the bootloader runs instead of the application when something asks the board into DFU again, when its bootloader entry pin reads grounded as the board resets, or when the board holds no whole application image to start
note: if the expansion\-header bridge is still fitted, remove it (pins 1 and 3 of P4 on an Ubertooth One), then unplug the board and plug it back in
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The first note appears only when \fB\-d\fR had written the whole image\. The second names the bootloader's three entry conditions, which are all there are\. The third is what to do about the one of them that repeats on every reset: the expansion\-header bridge described under MANUALLY ENTERING BOOTLOADER\.
.P
A run that wrote no image, \fB\-r\fR on its own or beside \fB\-u\fR, gets one more note after the one naming the causes, as the remedy for the third of them:
.IP "" 4
.nf
note: an interrupted download leaves the board without a whole image, so write one: \-d <image> \-r
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A board that has just taken a whole image is not in that state, so a run that wrote one is never told to write again\.
.P
The confirming wait is spent once per detach and only after the device has gone, so a board that never left the bootloader is reported as promptly as before\. A board that takes longer than the whole 1\.5 s, behind a hub or on a loaded host, is back on the bus after the confirming listing has already read it as gone, and is reported as detached\.
.P
If a transfer fails, in either direction, the tool closes the stale handle, reopens and claims the DFU device on the same physical path, restores its DFU state, and retries the whole transfer once from its first block\. A transfer that fails twice ends the command with exit status 1\. With \fB\-d \|\.\|\.\|\. \-r\fR, the image name and revision from the image's firmware info block are what the board must report back: the run does not return success until the application on that same path reports exactly both\. The image name matters because both images built from one tree carry the same revision, so a write of \fBbtbr\fR over \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR that did not take would otherwise pass on the revision alone\.
.P
With multiple Ubertooths, flash them sequentially using \fB\-U <serial>\fR and verify each device before starting the next\. DFU mode does not expose a serial number, so an explicit selector never adopts an unidentified device that was already in DFU when the command started\. Simultaneous flashing is not yet supported or validated\.
.P
\fB\-s\fR and \fB\-d\fR only read the file they are given, so a firmware image in a read\-only location can be signed or flashed\. \fB\-s <file\.bin>\fR writes \fB<file>\.dfu\fR, and refuses an input that already ends in a DFU suffix: signing it again would bury the first suffix in the firmware payload\. Nothing is written in that case\.
.P
\fB\-s\fR replaces \fB<file>\.dfu\fR only once the whole signed image is ready, writing it through a temporary file beside the target as \fB\-u\fR does for its dump (see \fIREADING FLASH BACK\fR)\. If the signing does not get that far, for any reason (an input it refuses, a \fB\-d\fR image refused in the same command, a read or a write that fails), an earlier \fB<file>\.dfu\fR is left untouched and none is created; whichever way the command ends on its own it leaves no temporary file behind, and only a signal that kills it can\. The image it writes is the one it always wrote: the input, unchanged, with the 16\-byte suffix appended, keeping the permissions of the file it replaced\.
.P
What happens after the signed file is in place is a separate matter\. \fB\-s\fR alongside \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-u\fR or \fB\-r\fR signs first and then goes to the board, so a command that fails there, with a device it cannot open or a flash that does not take, exits 1 with \fB<file>\.dfu\fR already written\.
.P
Writing it that way means \fB\-s\fR, like \fB\-u\fR, needs to be able to create a file in the output's directory, not just write the output itself: signing into a read\-only directory fails even where the \fB<file>\.dfu\fR it would replace is writable\. The new file is put in the target's place rather than written through it, so a \fB<file>\.dfu\fR that is a symlink, or one with other hard links to it, is replaced by the signed image instead of having the image written into what it points at\.
.P
Both read their whole file into memory, so what the file is comes first\. A directory, a FIFO or a device node is refused (\fBerror: <file> is not a regular file\fR): its length says nothing about what a read would return\. So is a file too large to be an image (\fBerror: <file> is <n> bytes; an Ubertooth image is at most 507920\fR)\. \fB\-d\fR takes at most 507920 bytes, which is the flash above the bootloader on the largest part this host knows plus the 16\-byte suffix, and \fB\-s\fR takes 507904, the same less the suffix it adds, so the file it writes is one \fB\-d\fR still accepts\. \fB\-s\fR names its own limit, and names what the limit is on, because the file it reads is the firmware rather than a whole image: \fBerror: <file> is <n> bytes; the firmware in an Ubertooth image is at most 507904\fR\. Either refusal ends the command with exit status 1 before any device is opened, and with \fB\-s\fR before the output file is created\.
.P
A command that gives both \fB\-s\fR and \fB\-d\fR checks both files it only reads before it creates anything, so an image it refuses costs the signed file nothing:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ ubertooth\-dfu \-s firmware\.bin \-d not\-an\-image\.dfu
Checking firmware signature
error: not\-an\-image\.dfu: Unknown DFU suffix length: 115; firmware will not be updated
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBfirmware\.dfu\fR is neither created nor replaced\.
.P
In the DFU bootloader, the TX, RX, and USR LEDs will turn on and off in sequence\. If this does not occur, you can try to manually force the device to boot into the bootloader\. Refer to \fIMANUALLY ENTERING BOOTLOADER\fR\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
Major modes:
.TP
\fB\-d <firmware\.dfu>\fR
Download \- write DFU file to device
.TP
\fB\-u <output\.bin>\fR
Upload \- read the application flash region, as much of it as the part has, into a raw file (see \fIREADING FLASH BACK\fR)\. A value naming standard output is refused while the command line is read, the lone \fB\-\fR and the names of descriptor 1 such as \fB/dev/stdout\fR alike (\fBerror: \-u cannot write the read\-back to stdout; give a file name\fR): the dump is written through a temporary file and rewound if the read has to be retried, and a pipe allows neither
.TP
\fB\-r\fR
Reset device after performing operation, or on its own to ask a board waiting in the bootloader to start the application it already has
.TP
\fB\-\-update\-bootloader\fR
Install the bootloader carried by the \fBbl_update\fR image given with \fB\-d\fR (see \fIUPDATING THE BOOTLOADER\fR)\. It needs \fB\-d\fR, and a board it writes it resets, so \fB\-r\fR alongside it changes nothing\. A board that already has that bootloader is left alone, still running its firmware and not reset; a board that is written is left in DFU, not running an application
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
With \fB\-\-update\-bootloader\fR, write without asking the board which bootloader it has\. It overrides the host's comparison and nothing else: the image makes the same comparison on the board, so a region that already matches is still not programmed\. What it costs is the application region, which the updater replaces either way\. On any other command line it is an error, because nothing else this tool does asks the board anything before it writes
.P
Miscellaneous:
.TP
\fB\-s <file\.bin>\fR
Add DFU suffix to binary firmware file
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select the application\-mode Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or a value\-taking option given twice is still reported\. It exits 0, except beside a \fB\-V\fR that could not read a board, whose status the command then carries\.
.SH "UPDATING THE BOOTLOADER"
A bootloader refuses USB writes below 0x4000, so it cannot replace itself: in earlier releases a new bootloader reached a board only over the serial programming interface or JTAG\. \fB\-\-update\-bootloader\fR installs one over USB, in one command:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-dfu \-\-update\-bootloader \-d bl_update\.dfu
ubertooth\-dfu \-d bluetooth_rxtx\.dfu \-r
ubertooth\-util \-B
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
From a checkout of this project, \fBinstall\.sh \-\-update\-bootloader \-\-flash\-device <index|serial|all>\fR runs all three lines for the board it names, one board at a time, and builds the images first\.
.P
\fBbl_update\.dfu\fR is an ordinary firmware image, built by \fBmake \-C firmware/bl_update\fR, that carries the 16 KiB of bootloader region to install\. This command writes it like any image and resets the board; the image programs the region through the part's own In\-Application Programming commands, reads it back, and resets into DFU, where this command waits for it\. That leaves the application region holding the updater, which is why the second line above is part of the procedure\. Before anything is written, this command prints the CRC32 the installed bootloader will report, and reads the bootloader region back as it stands; \fBubertooth\-util \-B\fR on the third line prints what the board actually has, so the two can be compared\.
.P
A board that already has the bootloader the image carries is left alone\. The running application is asked which bootloader it has, through the same request \fBubertooth\-util \-B\fR answers, before the board is asked for DFU:
.IP "" 4
.nf
The board already has this bootloader: ubertooth\-util \-B reports CRC32:0x7c52e323\.
Nothing was written, and the board is still running its firmware\.
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
It is the hashes that are compared and not the versions, because two builds of one bootloader can carry the same version\. Leaving the board alone saves more than the write to the bootloader region: the updater goes into the application region, so a run that went ahead would cost the board its firmware and leave it to be written again, for a bootloader that was already there\.
.P
\fB\-\-force\fR skips the question and writes\. It is not a way to program a region that already matches, because the image compares the region on the board too and declines; what it costs is the application region\. It is there for a run that wants the write made and the region read back whatever the board reports\.
.P
The question is not asked in three cases, and each of them says so rather than leaving it to be noticed\. A board adopted in the bootloader has no application to ask, so the run prints a \fBnote:\fR and writes as before\. \fB\-u\fR in the same command asks for a read\-back, which an early end would leave undone, so that too is a \fBnote:\fR and a write\. And a board whose firmware predates the request cannot answer, nor can one whose read fails; the run says so as a \fBwarning:\fR and installs the bootloader, which is what was asked for\. Skipping a write on a comparison that was never made would leave an old bootloader in place and report the board current\. (\fB\-\-force\fR is the fourth, and says nothing: it is the user turning the question off\.)
.P
Once the board is back in DFU the command reads the region again, because a board in DFU is also what an updater that never ran leaves behind: a bootloader whose entry pin reads grounded stays in DFU instead of starting the image, so nothing is installed and the board looks no different on the bus\. A region that changed into the bootloader the image carries is reported as installed; one that holds it without having changed, or without an earlier read to compare against, is reported as a region that holds it, which claims nothing about this run\. A region that provably does not hold it is an \fBerror:\fR and status 1: either it did not change at all, so nothing was installed, or it changed into neither bootloader, so the write did not finish\.
.P
Both reads go through the bootloader the same way, so a region that did not change is provable\. What they cannot settle is the lowest 512 bytes: the part covers them with either the user flash or its own boot ROM and the bootloader does not choose which, while the vector table and the version block both live there, so two builds of one bootloader can differ only inside them\. A run whose answer lies in those bytes \-\- the region did not change, and above them it matches the image \-\- is a \fBwarning:\fR and status 0 rather than a guess, because that is both a board which already had this bootloader and one which has another build of it and installed nothing\. Installing a bootloader that differs from the one on the board only in those bytes is therefore the case this check cannot confirm or refuse; \fBubertooth\-util \-B\fR after writing firmware again answers it\. A region that will not read back, before the write or after it, is reported the same way, except that a region which reads afterwards and holds the image's bootloader is confirmed whether or not the earlier read worked\.
.P
Have a serial adapter wired up and the programming entry pin reachable before you start\. The region is erased whole and the sector holding the vector table is programmed last, so an interruption leaves erased flash where that table belongs: the boot ROM refuses it and starts its own serial programmer, which the adapter reaches\. A read\-back the part will not confirm is the one failure reported once that sector is in flash, so the image erases it again: a board whose part takes that erase comes up in the serial programmer as well, rather than starting a bootloader nothing has confirmed\. A part that refuses the erase too is left as it was\. The exception is a loss of power inside the single 4 KiB program that writes the vector table, which leaves a checksum the boot ROM accepts in front of code that was never finished; recovering a board from that needs the entry pin held as well as the adapter\. Nothing here narrows that window\.
.P
Both the host and the image check the bootloader over the same bytes before any of it is programmed: that it is the whole 16 KiB region, that it carries a version block so the installed bootloader can report itself, that its vector table is one a part would start, and that the word at 0x2fc asks for no code read protection\. The host refuses with nothing written and no board touched\. The protection word is why the checks are made twice: CRP1 and CRP2 shut the debug interface, CRP3 shuts serial programming as well, NO_ISP turns the programming entry pin into an ordinary input, none of them can be undone from outside the part, and a board that took one cannot report that it did\.
.P
The image makes the same comparison on the board, so a bootloader region that already holds what the image carries is not programmed again, \fB\-\-force\fR or not: the image compares the region first and returns to DFU\.
.P
The image is what does the writing, so it writes whenever a board runs it: \fBubertooth\-dfu \-d bl_update\.dfu \-r\fR, with no \fB\-\-update\-bootloader\fR, programs the bootloader region as well, with none of the checks above\. Install it with \fB\-\-update\-bootloader\fR, and keep \fBbl_update\.dfu\fR out of the place you keep the images you flash by habit; a release archive ships it in \fBubertooth\-one\-firmware\-bin/bootloader\-update/\fR for that reason\.
.SH "READING FLASH BACK"
\fB\-u <output\.bin>\fR is not the inverse of \fB\-d\fR: it writes a raw dump of the application flash region, from 0x4000 to the end of the part's flash, with no DFU suffix, so \fB\-d\fR refuses the result\. The dump holds the image that was written (its own suffix included, padded with 0xff to the next 256\-byte block), followed by whatever the rest of the region contains, normally 0xff\.
.P
How long that is depends on the part, which differs between boards, so the command says what it is about to read, on standard error with the rest of its progress:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Reading back 114688 bytes, 0x4000\-0x1ffff: part 0x25011722 (LPC1754) has 128 KiB of flash
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A part with 128 KiB of flash gives 114688 bytes, one with 256 KiB gives 245760\. The size comes from the part identification number, which is only readable while the board is still running its application: when the board is already in the bootloader, or will not report it, the dump covers the 114688 bytes above the bootloader that the firmware is linked for, and the line says so in place of a part\. There is no option for a longer dump, so on a larger part that was already in the bootloader the shorter dump is all there is; let the tool enter DFU itself to get the whole region\. The assumption has a limit the other way too: a board with a smaller part than that, already in the bootloader so its part cannot be read, would still be asked for more flash than it has\. A bootloader built from this tree refuses such a request and carries on, but a board carries whatever bootloader was flashed on it, and an older one cannot survive being asked: it answers a read from any address below 512 KiB, and a read with no flash behind it faults it until the board is power\-cycled\.
.P
To compare a dump with the image it came from, compare the first bytes and check that the rest is padding, for example:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-dfu \-U <serial> \-u readback\.bin \-r
cmp \-n $(stat \-f%z bluetooth_rxtx\.dfu) bluetooth_rxtx\.dfu readback\.bin
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
On Linux use \fBstat \-c%s\fR\. The output file is only replaced once a complete dump has arrived: a read\-back that fails part\-way leaves an earlier file of the same name untouched, names the block that would not read, and exits 1\. Like \fB\-d\fR, it is retried once from the first block before the command gives up\. Whichever way the command ends on its own, including a \fB\-d\fR image it refuses, it leaves no temporary file beside the output; only a signal that kills it can\.
.SH "MANUALLY ENTERING BOOTLOADER"
If the device does not enter the bootloader when running the upload or download commands, it is possible to force the device to boot into that mode when it is inserted\. To do so, you will need to jumper two pins on the expansion header\.
.P
The expansion header is a 2x3 pin header between the two largest chips on the board, about halfway between the USB connector and antenna\. It is labeled "EXPAND" on the back side of the board\. Holding the board so USB is pointed up, on the front side of the board the expansion header looks like this:
.IP "" 4
.nf
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
6 + O | O | 3
  +\-\-\-+\-\-\-+
5 + O | O | 2
  +\-\-\-+\-\-\-+
4 + O | X | 1
  +\-\-\-+\-\-\-+
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Pin 1, marked X, is square\.
.P
With Ubertooth unplugged, bridge pins 1 and 3, then plug the device in while keeping the bridge in place\. Wait for the distinctive LED sequence or confirm that USB ID \fB1d50:6003\fR is present\. The bootloader samples the entry pin only during reset, so once DFU is active you may remove the bridge without exiting DFU\. Run \fBubertooth\-dfu\fR promptly because pin\-entry DFU times out after a few seconds if no DFU request begins; the board then resets, and with the bridge still fitted that reset lands in the bootloader again\.
.P
Remove the bridge before the final reset if you want the application firmware to boot\. If it remains installed, the next reset will sample the entry pin again and return to the bootloader, and \fB\-r\fR fails with \fBerror: detach failed: the board reset and came back in the bootloader\fR: the image is written, and the board is in DFU rather than running it\. If removing a live bridge is inconvenient, finish the flash, unplug the device, remove the bridge, and reconnect it; \fBubertooth\-util \-V\fR then reports the new firmware\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-dfu\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\. \fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so its usage text goes to standard output and it exits 0\.
.P
An option the tool does not know is refused the same way, as one line on standard error with no usage text after it: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR\. An option whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-d' requires a value\fR\.
.P
A file the tool cannot open, and an image it will not write, are each refused with one line on standard error, beginning \fBerror:\fR and naming the file\. The \fBChecking firmware signature\fR line that precedes an image refusal is progress, so it is on standard error as well and the two stay in order whatever either stream is piped into\.
.P
A command line with no function in it does nothing, so it is refused with \fBerror: nothing to do: give \-d to write an image, \-u to read one back, \-s to sign one, or \-r to reset\fR on standard error and exit status 1, before any device is opened\. A bare \fBubertooth\-dfu\fR, with no arguments at all, prints the usage text after that line; every other refused command line is the \fBerror:\fR line alone\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing, and leaves no temporary file: \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-u\fR and \fB\-d\fR name their file while the command line is read, and the files are opened only once every check has passed\. The files that are only read, the \fB\-d\fR image and the \fB\-s\fR input, are opened and checked first, since they are the last things that can refuse the command; neither output file is created until they have passed\. Whether \fB\-s\fR was given a \fB\.bin\fR is a check on the name, so it is still reported alongside \fB\-h\fR\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR: the image written and, with \fB\-r\fR, verified on the board that came back; the dump complete and in place; the signed \fB<file>\.dfu\fR written; the board out of the bootloader; the bootloader installed, a board that already had the bootloader it was asked to install, or a bootloader update whose outcome the board could not be made to show\.
.P
1 for everything else, and there is no other status\. That covers a command line the tool will not take or that has nothing to do, a file it cannot read or create, an image it refuses, a selected board that is missing or that something else is using, a bootloader that will not answer or will not become idle, a transfer that failed both its attempts, a reset that left the board in DFU or brought it straight back into the bootloader, a bootloader update whose region is provably not the bootloader it was asked to install \-\- whether because it did not change at all or because it changed into neither \-\- and a verification after the reset that did not end in the image's own name and revision\. That last one includes a board that did not come back on USB at all: the write had completed by then, but the command cannot say the board runs what was written, so it is not reported as a success\. The line it prints says which of those happened\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries the usage text for \fB\-h\fR, and otherwise what the command read back off the board\. From \fB\-d <image> \-r\fR that is one line:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Verified firmware revision: 2026\-11\-DEV1+22
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
That is the version the board reported once it was running the new firmware, so a script can read it on its own; the image name the verification also reads is checked rather than printed, because the \fBImage: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR line already said which one was written\. The version names the release the image was built from, not the commit, so two builds the same distance past a release carry the same one: this line says the board is running that release and that image, and telling two particular builds apart means comparing the file's checksum\. From \fB\-\-update\-bootloader\fR it is what the board now holds and what to do next:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Bootloader installed: ubertooth\-util \-B will report CRC32:0x7c52e323\.
The board is in DFU\. Write your firmware again (ubertooth\-dfu \-d bluetooth_rxtx\.dfu \-r)\.
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A region that holds that bootloader without this run being shown to have put it there \-\- it did not change, or it could not be read before the write \-\- says that instead, because a run that wrote nothing should not read as one that did:
.IP "" 4
.nf
The bootloader region holds this bootloader: ubertooth\-util \-B will report CRC32:0x7c52e323\.
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A run that could not read the region back prints the \fBThe board is in DFU\fR line alone, and names \fBubertooth\-util \-B\fR in it\.
.P
A board that already had that bootloader when the command started is not written at all, and says so without mentioning DFU, because it never went there:
.IP "" 4
.nf
The board already has this bootloader: ubertooth\-util \-B reports CRC32:0x7c52e323\.
Nothing was written, and the board is still running its firmware\.
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Nothing else is ever written to standard output: the dump of \fB\-u\fR goes to the file it names, and the signed image of \fB\-s\fR to \fB<file>\.dfu\fR, and a run that only writes firmware or resets the board says nothing there at all\.
.P
Everything else is on standard error, so a script reading that one line is not fed the progress of a flash that takes a few seconds:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Checking firmware signature
Image: bluetooth_rxtx 2026\-11\-DEV1+22 (API:<version>)
DFU expected revision: 2026\-11\-DEV1+22
The image needs 31488 bytes, 0x4000\-0xbaff: part 0x25011722 (LPC1754) has 128 KiB of flash
Switching selected device to DFU mode\|\.\|\.\|\.
\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\|\.\.
Detached
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fB\-u\fR prints \fBReading back <n> bytes, \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR there in the same way, with one dot per 256\-byte block read, and \fBDFU +<seconds>s: <stage>\fR lines trace what the command spent its time on\.
.P
The errors this tool prints itself are one line each beginning \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: detach failed: the board is still in DFU\fR, \fBerror: upload failed: block 164 was not read\fR, \fBerror: no bootloader was installed: the region read back as 0x\|\.\|\.\|\. both before and after this run, and that is not the bootloader bl_update\.dfu carries, which would report CRC32:0x\|\.\|\.\|\.\fR, \fBerror: \-\-force goes with \-\-update\-bootloader, whose check against the bootloader the board already has it overrides; nothing else this tool does asks the board before writing\fR, \fBerror: the bootloader region holds neither bootloader: \|\.\|\.\|\. so the write did not finish\fR, \fBerror: firmware update failed after 2 attempt(s): \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR and \fBerror: could not read the DFU state: the board's firmware does not support it (stalled, libusb \-9)\fR\. That last one is the shared wording for a request the board refused, and it comes without the firmware API version note described in ubertooth(7): the bootloader this tool talks to runs no application firmware to have a version\. Lines beginning \fBwarning:\fR are the ones about a part this host cannot identify, about an image built for another API version, about a board that could not say which bootloader it has, so a bootloader update could not tell whether it already had the one asked for, and about a bootloader region that did not change and holds what the image carries except in the lowest 512 bytes, which is the one outcome of an update that cannot be told apart\. Lines beginning \fBnote:\fR say more about something the command has just reported: today a board that came back in the bootloader, a board waiting in the bootloader that a selection could not name, a board that never came back from a bootloader update and may be in the part's serial programmer, a bootloader update that installed nothing, one whose result could not be read back, and a bootloader update with \fB\-u\fR alongside it, which is not asked whether the board already has the bootloader\. libubertooth's own lines read the same way, including the ones it ends up printing for a libusb failure nothing could name (\fBerror: Input/Output Error (libusb \-1)\fR) and for a selection that found nothing (\fBerror: no Ubertooth devices found\fR)\. A refusal from it can run to more than one line, the lines after the first being its detail; see ubertooth(7)\.
.P
The \fB\-d \|\.\|\.\|\. \-r\fR verification has more than one way not to pass, and they do not mean the same thing, so each has its own line:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: the firmware was written, but the board did not come back on USB: power\-cycle it and read its revision with ubertooth\-util \-V before flashing it again
error: the firmware was written, but the board came back and would not open, so the flash was not checked: read its revision with ubertooth\-util \-V once nothing else is using it
error: the firmware was written, but the host's USB bus could not be read, so the flash was not checked: read the board's revision with ubertooth\-util \-V
error: the board came back running 2026\-11\-DEV1+21, not 2026\-11\-DEV1+22: the firmware was not written, so flash it again
error: the board came back running the btbr firmware, not the bluetooth_rxtx image that was written: the firmware was not written, so flash it again
error: the board came back but would not report its firmware revision, so the flash was not checked: read it with ubertooth\-util \-V
error: the board came back running 2026\-11\-DEV1+22, the revision that was written, but would not say which firmware image it is, so which of this tree's images it runs is unchecked
error: the device that came back is not on the flashed board's USB path, so the flash was not checked: read the board's revision with ubertooth\-util \-V
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Two of those say the write did not take and ask for another flash: the one that names another revision, and the one that names the other image\. The first three lines are boards whose flash already holds the new firmware: the whole image went in and the board was reset before it stopped answering, so what they need is a \fBubertooth\-util \-V\fR, not another flash\. The second of them is a board that is on the bus and will not open, usually because something else is using it or this user has no permission to it, so a power cycle is not what it is waiting for; the third is a board that was never looked at, because the host's own USB bus could not be listed\. The rest leave the flash unchecked rather than failed\. Whichever line is printed, the stage trace carries the raw codes behind it on its \fBpost\-reset application verification failed: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR line\.
.P
An image whose firmware info block reports an API version other than these tools' own is not refused for that, only warned about: the tools and the board have to report the same one, and this command is the way back from a board that does not\. It is a warning rather than a refusal for that reason\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
warning: older\.dfu: this image reports API version 1\.07 in its firmware info block, and these tools report <version>; the tools and the board have to report the same one
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
What the warning reports is what the file says about itself\. The other tools read the board's USB \fBbcdDevice\fR rather than this block, so the warning does not promise what they will make of the board afterwards\. Both images this tree builds put the same version in both places, so the two agree today; nothing in the format makes them have to\.
